To implement a multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a `Dialogue` class to represent a dialogue with multiple choices. This class should have the following attributes:
   - `text`: The main text of the dialogue.
   - `choices`: An array of strings representing the choices the player can make.
   - `responses`: An array of strings representing the responses to each choice.
   - `currentChoiceIndex`: The index of the currently selected choice.

2. Implement a method in the `Dialogue` class to display the dialogue to the player. This method should:
   - Print the main text of the dialogue.
   - Print the choices and their corresponding numbers (e.g., "1. Choice 1", "2. Choice 2", etc.).
   - Wait for the player to input a number corresponding to one of the choices.
   - If the input is valid, set `currentChoiceIndex` to the index of the chosen choice.
   - If the input is invalid, print an error message and call the method again.

3. Implement a method in the `Dialogue` class to get the player's choice. This method should return the response corresponding to the currently selected choice.

Here's an example implementation of the `Dialogue` class:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Dialogue {
    private String text;
    private String[] choices;
    private String[] responses;
    private int currentChoiceIndex;

    public Dialogue(String text, String[] choices, String[] responses) {
        this.text = text;
        this.choices = choices;
        this.responses = responses;
        this.currentChoiceIndex = -1;
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(text);
        System.out.println("Choose an option:");
        for (int i = 0; i < choices.length; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + choices[i]);
        }

        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int choice;
        do {
            choice = scanner.nextInt();
            if (choice < 1 || choice > choices.length) {
                System.out.println("Invalid choice. Please try again.");
            }
        } while (choice < 1 || choice > choices.length);

        currentChoiceIndex = choice - 1;
    }

    public String getChoice() {
        if (currentChoiceIndex == -1) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("No choice has been selected.");
        }
        return responses[currentChoiceIndex];
    }
}
```

